{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791570572,"nonce":"/8yTDLmzNRC23XQ2BzN34Mgk5Uk=","title":"Host-run effects: run another language's effect code under the reactive graph's dependency tracking","message":"Requested by the ashui session for ashui (Haxe) and blinc_ts (TypeScript), which wrap every effect in a boxed Rust closure that calls back into the host. In blinc_ts that is about 1.15 KB per effect and one host call per due effect on every flush. Tracking only existed inside ReactiveGraph::run_effect, so a host could not run its own code tracked.\n\nBuilt (parts 1 to 3 of the request):\n1. ReactiveGraph::create_host_effect: an effect node with no Rust body. flush_effects never runs it.\n2. take_due_host_effects: a pull, not a callback, in the order they became due. It cannot be a callback because flush runs under the graph mutex and the host must not be called there. The effect stays dirty until it is run, so it is not handed out twice.\n3. begin_effect / end_effect: a tracking scope that can span a call into the host. Reads through the graph record dependencies; writes through the Signal API wait. Scopes nest: begin saves the active tracking and end restores it, and end pops up to the matching id, so a missed inner end cannot wedge the stack and ending twice does nothing. begin_host_effect / end_host_effect do the same on the global graph, and end drains the deferred writes after the graph lock is released.\n\nNot built: host-run derived values (part 4). Derived is pull-based: a dirty derived recomputes inside get(), so a host derived would need a call into the host from within a read, which is the closure being removed. The SDKs keep computeds, or use a host effect that writes a signal and accept the glitch window.\n\nDecisions:\n- A host scope holds no lock and sets no in-flight pointer. The existing deferral works through a thread-local raw pointer read without a lock, sound only because flush holds the mutex; reusing it across a host call would let another thread mutate the graph meanwhile. Reads take the ordinary locked path; only the write deferral is flagged (defers_writes, separate from is_in_flush, which three other sites use to decide whether the pointer exists).\n- A signal written while a scope is open, before the effect is subscribed to it, re-queues the effect at end_effect, so the change is not lost.\n- The scope belongs to one thread. Both SDKs are single-threaded on the graph (blinc_ts owns a !Send graph in a RefCell, ashui uses the process-global one from the main thread); end_effect debug-asserts it ends on the thread that began.\n\nFixes found on the way, all of which a host scope needed:\n- run_effect took the tracking buffer to None when it finished, so a closure effect run inside a scope (create_effect flushes at once) silently dropped the host's reads. It now saves and restores the outer tracking and the in-flight pointer.\n- get_derived did the same when it recomputed a derived, and a read of a clean, cached derived never gave the reader the derived's signals, unlike the in-flight read. Both now record into the reader's tracking.\n\nTests: 17 in reactive::host_effects, including nesting, an abandoned inner scope, a closure effect and a derived run inside a scope, disposal while running, a write made during the run, and global-graph write deferral. Three mutations were each caught by the test aimed at them.","files":null}]}